WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:07.780
6 chips, and 6 answers to the same question. Who schedules the movement of data, and where

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:14.620
does that data live? On the left is our own SoC 1.1, as it runs today. 8 cores queuing

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:22.060
behind a single DMA engine. Next, Mobileye EyeQ, where VLIW cores share an L2 through a

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:28.420
multi-channel DMA. Better than one engine, still memory shared. Then NVIDIA's Orin,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:34.780
which answers the question with brute force. 2048 CUDA cores, and per streaming multiprocessor

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:43.300
shared memory, at 60 watts. At the far right, Tesla's Dojo D1. 354 cores, each with its

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:49.580
own private SRAM, and no DRAM on the die at all. The most extreme position on the spectrum,

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.820
and a project Tesla disbanded in 2025. Halo sits near it, with a distributed memory fabric,

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.620
and no external DRAM, extremely efficient across a narrow band of workloads. Now watch

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:08.580
the middle. Per core DMA against a shared L2 cache, an engine for every core, memory still

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.500
shared, is where the roadmap goes, and it is where most of the industry already sits.
